### Materials And Mechanic Adjustments

<!-- Explain which materials are created, adapted, or reused and why they work
for this prototype. Describe how rules, values, layouts, or pieces can be changed
between playthroughs without rebuilding the whole prototype. Focus on usable,
readable materials and actual design choices. -->
[The prototype uses simple physical materials to keep the game affordable and easy to build. The original token ball was replaced with a crumpled paper ball because it was easier to shoot. The field was also redesigned after feedback that it felt too stiff. The field and ball can be adjusted during future tests to improve movement and gameplay.]

## Intended Players

<!-- Identify the intended audience's relevant interests and play habits.
Explain why this game's decisions, theme, or intended experience would appeal
to those players. Connect the audience to specific design choices. -->
[Retro Kick is intended for people who enjoy soccer, friendly competition, and simple physical games. It is designed for players who want a quick game that is easy to understand but still requires control and skill. The finger-controlled movement makes the game different from traditional soccer.]

## Accessibility And Inclusivity

<!-- Explain concrete ways people with different abilities can perceive,
understand, and use the game's actual interactions. Explain how these choices
preserve the intended gameplay and can carry into the later digital version.
Choose considerations relevant to this game; a generic feature list is not enough. -->
[The game uses simple rules and physical pieces that are easy to understand. Players do not need advanced gaming experience or special equipment. The field should have clear boundaries and visible goals so players can understand where to move the ball. Future versions could use larger goals and a more visible ball to make gameplay easier to follow.]

## Unique Selling Points (USP)

<!-- Unique selling points are the distinctive design choices that give intended
players a reason to choose this game. Explain those choices through a relevant
comparison with an existing game. Connect the differences to the intended
players' experience; support the comparison with concrete design details. -->
players' experience; support the comparison with concrete design details.
Explain why the difference would make play more interesting to those players. -->
[Retro Kick combines the familiar idea of soccer with finger-controlled physical gameplay. Unlike digital soccer games such as FIFA, players physically interact with the ball and field instead of controlling characters with a controller. This makes the game hands-on, simple, and competitive. The low-cost materials also make it easy to recreate and play.]

